00:00:12.541 --> 00:00:16.910
confirmed that he was indeed connected to
Russian intelligence, which by the way, should

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.440
not come as a surprise to us.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.160
We actually suspected that for a long time
and it was confirmed by the Mueller report.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.390
You might or might not remember, so let's
reset.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:33.150
You may recall that Julian Assange spent years
holed up in the Ecuadorian embassy in London

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.300
to avoid arrest and extradition.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.840
Eventually we learned that the Ecuadorian
embassy wasn't particularly thrilled with

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.770
Assad continuing to be there.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:47.160
There were ideas that his hygiene and something
about his cats or something was at least part

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.900
of the reason, but ultimately they told them
that it's time for him to go, but we've now

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:58.290
learned that they were at least aware of another
factor on the basis of an investigation that

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:03.850
the Ecuadorian embassy conducted a thanks
to a Spanish investigation firm, which is

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.060
something that Robert Mueller also told us,
and that is that Julian Assange is tied to

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.140
Russian intelligence.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:16.259
Now, the Mueller report concluded this already
a wikileaks helped the Russians undermine

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:18.329
the United States election.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:24.780
In 2016, Assad was effectively working with
a go between, uh, between, uh, wikileaks and

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.790
Russian intelligence.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.850
And ultimately Ecuador realize the exact same
thing.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.929
They ended up hiring a Spanish company to
investigate Julian Assange.

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:38.079
Their report was obtained by CNN and it includes
information about at least seven meetings

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:42.399
in June of 2016, which was a key moment in
everything that was going on with the Trump

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:43.399
campaign.

00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:44.400
And Russian help.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.040
Uh, there were, uh, a number of meetings in
June of 2016 that Julian Assange had with

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.579
Russians and others with ties to the Kremlin.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.520
Five meetings with senior staffers from our
tea.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.219
That's the Russian state television program.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:05.450
And that includes receiving a USB drive from
rts, London bureau chief Nikolai Bogo chicken.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.119
I don't know if I'm pronouncing that name
correctly.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:13.070
So the biggest news here is that this surprises
anybody because all along, you know, Julian

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:18.140
Assange would claim all along that wikileaks
is not politically motivated in the sense

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:22.159
of partisanship they aren't for or against
any party or candidate.

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:28.110
They don't time releases of information in
order to have any particular partisan political

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.110
effect.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.390
But over time we started to see patterns.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:33.640
We started to suspect that that wasn't the
case.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:39.830
A Sanches denial of a Russian connection was
really narrowly worded.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:45.730
I'm going from memory, but as Sanjiv said,
something like our source is not a Russian

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.331
government actor or something like that, which
of course left open the possibility that it

00:02:51.331 --> 00:02:57.760
was some kind of informal actor or a go-between
of some kind that could have any nationality

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.200
that was functioning as a go between, uh,
between Russian intelligence and wikileaks.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.280
And then Robert Mueller figured out that it
actually was exactly in this way, despite

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.770
it being relatively under publicized.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:14.359
You know, when the Mueller report came out,
when the bar letter was published, the narrative

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:17.780
that the Trumpists were pushing was no collusion,
no obstruction.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:23.299
They went on a national exoneration tour,
uh, and just talking about the obstruction

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:29.930
collusion stuff, sort of smothered the findings
by Robert Mueller that, uh, wikileaks was,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.489
uh, Russia connected in this entire thing.

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:37.430
Now, there are some people who are reacting
and saying, okay, this is just one instance

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:43.400
where wikileaks acted politically in cahoots
with Russia, but basically wikileaks is a

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.659
nonpartisan actor and they don't make decisions
that are meant to favor any particular people

00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:50.689
in power, but certainly not Russia.

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:52.230
That is very, very wrong.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:57.879
You might recall that when wikileaks got the
leaks of those infamous Panama papers, those

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:04.830
papers included information that exposed corruption
